<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pakistan</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pakistan is a country in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-asia">South Asia</a>, covering the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus">Indus</a> valley and the north-western frontier of the subcontinent. In antiquity much of its territory belonged to successive empires. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chandragupta-maurya">Chandragupta Maurya</a> brought the region, along with parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>, into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maurya-empire">Maurya Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pakistan.jpg" alt="Pakistan" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Zscout370 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Pakistan.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Pakistan is bounded by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabian-sea">Arabian Sea</a> on the south and shares land borders with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> to the east, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a> to the west, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> to the southwest and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> to the northeast. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wakhan">Wakhan Corridor</a>, a narrow strip of Afghanistan, separates it from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tajikistan">Tajikistan</a>. It lies where the Indian and Eurasian tectonic plates meet, a region of high seismic activity.</p><p>The country is divided into three major geographic areas: the northern highlands, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus">Indus River</a> plain and the Balochistan Plateau. The highlands include the Karakoram, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hindu-kush">Hindu Kush</a> and Pamir ranges and five of the fourteen peaks over 8,000 metres, among them K2 at 8,611 metres. The 1,609 km Indus River and its tributaries cross the country from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashmir">Kashmir</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabian-sea">Arabian Sea</a> and sustain the alluvial plains of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sindh">Sindh</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The earliest known <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stone-tools">stone tools</a> in the region, about 2 million years old, were found in the Soan Valley. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> site of Mehrgarh dates from 7000 to 4300 BCE, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus-valley-civilisation">Indus Valley Civilisation</a> included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohenjo-daro">Mohenjo-daro</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harappa">Harappa</a>. After its decline, semi-nomadic Indo-European Aryans migrated into the subcontinent around 2000 BCE, and the Gandhara civilisation later flourished at the crossroads of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-east">Middle East</a>.</p><p>The western regions became part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a> around 517 BCE, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> conquered the region in 326 BCE, defeating King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porus">Porus</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jhelum">Jhelum</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maurya-empire">Mauryas</a> ruled from 322 BCE to 185 BCE, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashoka">Ashoka</a> introduced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indo-greek-kingdom">Indo-Greek Kingdom</a> founded by Demetrius of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bactria">Bactria</a> included Gandhara and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a>, and Greco-Buddhist culture flourished under Menander. Later powers included the Kushan, the Ghaznavids, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughals</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-raj">British Raj</a> from 1858 to 1947.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Pakistan gained independence in 1947 through the partition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a>, which was accompanied by mass migration and loss of life. It adopted a republican constitution in 1956, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-pakistan">East Pakistan</a> seceded as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangladesh">Bangladesh</a> in 1971 after a nine-month civil war. The 1973 constitution established a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parliamentary-democracy">parliamentary system</a>. Military rule governed in 1958 to 1971, 1977 to 1988 and 1999 to 2008, and the first successful democratic transition took place in May 2013.</p><p>The 2023 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> counted 247,541,947 people, and the population grew more than sevenfold between 1951 and 2023. The country is ethnically and linguistically diverse and has a very young population, with a median age of 19. Its legislature has a 96-member Senate and a 336-member National Assembly.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pakistan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>India, <b>Pakistan</b>, Bangladesh and Myanmar inherited its borders, provinces, armies, railways, legal codes and civil services; the Indian Administrative Service and the Indian Penal Code descend directly from colonial institutions, and English became a language of government, law and higher education across the region.</blockquote><a href="/empire/british-raj">British Raj · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>At its peak it ruled present-day Afghanistan, much of <b>Pakistan</b>, parts of Iran, eastern Turkmenistan and north-western India.</blockquote><a href="/empire/durrani-empire">Durrani Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The name <b>Pakistan</b> was coined in January 1933 by the activist Choudhry Rahmat Ali in a pamphlet, Now or Never, as an acronym of Panjab, Afghania, Kashmir, Sindh and Baluchistan; in Persian and Urdu it also means 'the land of the pure'.</blockquote><a href="/empire/islamic-republic-of-pakistan">Islamic Republic of Pakistan · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>In a war and treaty with Seleucus I Nicator around 305–303 BCE, he gained Arachosia, Gedrosia and the Paropamisadae, covering much of modern <b>Pakistan</b> and parts of Afghanistan, in return for 500 war elephants, and received the Greek envoy Megasthenes at his court.</blockquote><a href="/empire/maurya-empire">Maurya Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Independence came on 15 August 1947 together with partition: the Muslim-majority provinces of the north-west and east became <b>Pakistan</b>, and the division was accompanied by communal violence that killed hundreds of thousands and displaced some 15 million people.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-india">Republic of India · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>A mutual defence pact with <b>Pakistan</b> followed in September 2025, and the kingdom has said it would seek nuclear weapons if Iran acquires them.</blockquote><a href="/empire/saudi-arabia">Saudi Arabia · Military</a></li></ul></section>
